# Copy to .env and fill in. Vite only exposes keys prefixed VITE_.
#
# Formspree form ID for the contact form (brief §B.5). Create a form at
# formspree.io — the free tier is enough — and paste the ID from the endpoint
# it gives you: https://formspree.io/f/XXXXXXXX
#
# Without this the contact form still renders, but submitting shows the
# WhatsApp/email fallback instead of pretending to send. Set it before launch.
VITE_FORMSPREE_ID=

# --- First-party analytics (optional) ---------------------------------------
#
# Our own Supabase project. There is no Google Analytics and no third-party
# tracking script on this site; if these two are unset the site works exactly as
# before and records nothing at all — analytics.js checks isConfigured() before
# every send, so an unconfigured build is a silent no-op rather than an error.
#
# BEFORE THESE DO ANYTHING, run the migration:
#   supabase/migrations/20260729000000_analytics.sql
# It creates the events table, the RLS policies, and the passphrase-checking
# function the dashboard reads through. Change the CHANGE-ME-BEFORE-RUNNING
# passphrase literal in that file first.
#
# Both are inlined by Vite at BUILD time. Setting them on a running container
# does nothing — the same trap VITE_FORMSPREE_ID has.
#
# The anon key is public by design: it ships inside the JS bundle and anyone can
# read it. That is safe here only because of the RLS in the migration — anon may
# INSERT events and may not SELECT them, and the dashboard's passphrase is
# verified inside Postgres. Never put the service_role key here.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
